# API and auth

## Compose the guarantee, do not restate it

| Builder | Guarantees |
| --- | --- |
| `publicProcedure` | nothing |
| `protectedProcedure` | a signed-in `context.user` |
| `orgProcedure` | an active `context.organizationId` |
| `requirePermission({ post: ["delete"] })` | the caller's role grants it |

New permissions go in `packages/authz`, never inline in a handler. Authorization
is asked of Better Auth (`auth.api.hasPermission`) rather than re-derived from
the session — the role lives on the member row, and a second reader is a second
source of truth.

## Every query is scoped by organization

```ts
// ✅ the permission check and the scope are both required
await db.delete(post).where(and(eq(post.id, input.id), eq(post.organizationId, context.organizationId)));

// ❌ an admin of one tenant can delete another tenant's row by guessing an id
await db.delete(post).where(eq(post.id, input.id));
```

## Anything that changes the session goes through `authClient`, not oRPC

Creating an organization, switching organizations, signing in or out, inviting,
accepting an invitation. Better Auth's own route is the only caller that can put
the refreshed session cookie on the response. Proxied through an oRPC procedure
the write succeeds, the cookie does not change, and the signed cookie cache
keeps serving the previous value for minutes — a bug that looks correct in
review and is wrong in the browser.

oRPC is for the product's own domain.

## Database hooks run at surprising times

Better Auth queues `create.after` hooks until the surrounding transaction
finishes. At sign-up a user hook has not run by the time the session row is
written, which is why the active organization is resolved from the
session-creation hook instead.
